Numerical Methods
In this way 13.4.1-13.4.7 can be used together with 13.1.27 to extend Table 13.1 to the range
\[-10\leq a\leq 10,\ -10\leq b\leq 10,\ -10\leq x\leq 10.\]
This extension of ten units in any direction is possible with the loss of about 1S. All the recurrence relations are stable except i) if a<0, b<0 and a|>|b|, x>0, or ii) b<a, b<0, |b-a|>|b|, x<0, when the oscillations may become large, especially if |x| also is large.
Neither interpolation nor the use of recurrence relations should be attempted in the strips b=-n\pm.1 where the function is very large numerically. In particular M(a, b, x) cannot be evaluated in the neighborhood of the points a=-m, b=-n, m\leq n, as near these points small changes in a, b or x can produce very large changes in the numerical value of M(a, b, x).
At the point (-1,-1,x), M(a, b, x) is undefined. When a==1, M(-1, b, x)=1-\frack zero for all x. Hence \lim_{b\to-1}M(-1, b, x)=1+x. But M(b, b, x)=e^k for all x, when a=b. Hence \lim_{b\to-1}M(b, b, x)=e^k. In the first case b\to-1 along the line a=-1, and in the second case b\to-1 along the line a=b.
